paramedics like caitlyn have dedicated  their lives to saving others  so it seems unbelievable that during a  global health crisis  they should have to deal with abuse and  assault from the very people they're  trying to help  i attended a male on shift had a head  injury  he was really aggressive towards us as a  crew and he spat on me  which landed on my face and went in my  eye i  after this had to go to hospital have my  eye irrigated with water  and also have make sure i update my  vaccinations to make sure that i didn't  catch any  fluid-borne viruses to make sure that i  was safe  um it just made me feel really i felt  quite disgusted in myself i felt really  dirty  sky news can reveal that during the  covid crisis ambulance workers have been  subjected to hundreds of violent attacks  10 of the uk's 13 ambulance services  responded to our freedom of information  request  and according to their figures they've  been over 1600  physical assaults on staff between  january and july  149 sexual assaults were recorded  as well as almost 100 incidents  involving weapons  from firearms and stun guns to razors  and knives  a situation experienced by clinical care  assistant rob  as he dealt with an overdose patient me  and  this patient ended up on the bed  fighting cheek to cheek  had a bruising on my cheek he was  scratching i'd use practice on me  belly in that and he was kicking off and  then all of a sudden he dived straight  between both of us  uh went down to this cupboard we didn't  see it there he was in  uh pulled out a knife about four or five  inches knife  and jumped over that and it wasn't your  like shadow box if he did he tried to  stab us both the gmb  represents more ambulance workers than  any other union  it's backing a proposal for those  sentences to be increased  to two years the other thing that's  happening in the ambulance trusts up and  down the country  is that they're being they're trialing  body cameras  like the police have to wear in order to  try and regulate the behavior of the  people that they're trying to help  i mean it's unthinkable that whilst  facing down a pandemic  whilst dealing with the trauma that the  ambulance staff see on a daily basis  they're also now witness to so many  assaults from the public  the nhs says it'll report anyone who  attacks or abuses their staff  and support any subsequent prosecutions  but with the number of incidents  continuing to rise  many of their staff would like to see  more being done to protect them  as they try to protect us fraser moored  sky news
 

